## 1. Introduction

Neutron stars (NSs) are the end product of the evolution of stars of moderate mass (\\sim 7\\,\\mathrm{M}_{\\odot}-20\\,\\mathrm{M}_{\\odot}, depending on the metallicity). About 20 NS-NS binaries have been observed in the radio band in our Milky Way Galaxy alone (e.g., Martinez et al., 2017; Tauris et al., 2017; Cameron et al., 2018; Lynch et al., 2018; Stovall et al., 2018; Ridolfi et al., 2019), some of which might have been formed in, and then ejected from, dense star clusters (Andrews and Mandel, 2019). Gravitational wave (GW) detectors promise to observe hundreds of merging NS-NS and BH-NS (black hole-neutron star) binaries. Two NS-NS binaries have been observed so far by the LIGO/Virgo collaboration, including the most massive NS-NS binary ever observed (Abbott et al., 2017; Abbott et al., 2020). The merger rate in the local Universe has been estimated \\sim 250-2810\\,\\mathrm{Gpc^{-3}yr^{-1}}. No BH-NS binaries have been confirmed either through radio observations or GW emission1, with a LIGO/Virgo 90% upper limit of \\sim 610\\,\\mathrm{Gpc^{-3}yr^{-1}} on the merger rate (Abbott et al., 2019). The recent discovery of GW190814, a coalescence of a \\sim 23\\,\\mathrm{M}_{\\odot} BH with a \\sim 2.6\\,\\mathrm{M}_{\\odot} compact object, could be the either be a binary BH merger (with a BH in the mass gap) or the first detected BH-NS merger, with a rate of \\sim 1-23\\,\\mathrm{Gpc^{-3}yr^{-1}}(Abbott et al., 2020). In any case, NS-NS and BH-NS mergers result of a particular interest since they are followed by a short gamma-ray burst, which can reveal the details of the explosion mechanism and neutron star structure (e.g., Narayan et al., 1992; Berger, 2014; D'Orazio et al., 2016).

Footnote 1: affiliation: Possible candidates can be found at [https://gracedb.ligo.org/](https://gracedb.ligo.org/).

Several different astrophysical channels have been proposed to form merging compact objects. Possibilities include isolated binary evolution through a common envelope phase (Belczynski et al., 2016; Kruckow et al., 2018) or through chemically homogeneous evolution (de Mink and Mandel, 2016; Marchant et al., 2016), mergers in star clusters (Askar et al., 2017; Banerjee, 2018; Fragione and Kocsis, 2018; Rodriguez et al., 2018; Kremer et al., 2020), Kozai-Lidov (KL) mergers of binaries in galactic nuclei (Antonini and Perets, 2012; Petrovich and Antonini, 2017; Fragione et al., 2018; Grishin et al., 2018), in stellar triple (Antonini et al., 2017; Silsbee and Tremaine, 2017; Fragione and Loeb, 2019, 2019; Fragione et al., 2020) and quadruple systems (Fragione and Kocsis, 2019; Liu and Lai, 2019), GW capture events in galactic nuclei (O'Leary et al., 2009; Rasskazov and Kocsis, 2019), and mergers in active galactic nuclei accretion disks (Bartos et al., 2017; Stone et al., 2017; Secunda et al., 2019). Each model can potentially account for a significant fraction of the overall observed population, predicting a rate of \\sim\\,1-100\\,\\mathrm{Gpc^{-3}yr^{-1}} in the local Universe. With hundreds of confirmed events from future detections, the statistical contribution of different channels could be disentangled by using the distributions of their masses, spins, eccentricities, and redshifts (O'Leary et al., 2016; Gondan et al., 2018).

In the dense environment of star clusters, the frequent three- and four-body encounters can efficiently drive binary BHs to merger. A number of studies have shown that merging BH-BH binaries are formed at high enough rates in star clusters to potentially explain the LIGO/Virgo detection rate (e.g., Fragione and Kocsis, 2018; Rodriguez et al., 2018, 2018; Samsing and D'Orazio, 2018; Choksi et al., 2019; Kremer et al., 2019; Samsing et al., 2019).

Recently, the possible contribution of star clusters to NSNS and BH-NS mergers has become under scrutiny. Ye et al. (2020) estimated that the merger rate of NS-NS and BH-NS binaries in globular clusters is \\sim 0.02-0.04 Gpc{}^{-3} yr{}^{-1}, about \\sim 3-4 orders of magnitude smaller than the inferred LIGO/Virgo rates. The incapacity of catalyzing these mergers has to be ascribed to the BH-burning process (Kremer et al., 2020), where stellar BHs dominate the cluster cores and prevent the mass segregation of NSs (Fragione et al., 2018; Ye et al., 2019). As a result, the rates of dynamical interactions involving NSs are reduced and merging binaries containing NSs cannot be efficiently formed. A similar result was found by Belczynski et al. (2018), who derived a NS-NS merger rate from globular clusters of \\sim 0.05 Gpc{}^{-3} yr{}^{-1}. Similar results were obtained by Bae et al. (2014), Clausen et al. (2013), and Arca Sedda (2020) for globular and nuclear star clusters.

In this paper, we use for the first time high-precision self-consistent N-body simulations of young massive and open clusters to study the origin and dynamical evolution of NSs. Our direct N-body simulations are performed with the state-of-the-art collisional evolution code Nbody7(Aarseth, 2003, 2012), with the most up-to-date prescriptions for single and binary stellar evolution Banerjee et al. (2020). To this end, we use a suite of a set of 65 N-body simulations, presented for the first time in Banerjee (2020), encompassing different cluster masses, initial fractions of primordial binaries, and metallicities. For the first time, our findings show that also young massive and open clusters are not an efficient factory of BH-NS and NS-NS mergers, producing a merger rate about \\sim 3-4 orders of magnitude smaller than the LIGO/Virgo rates.

Our paper is organized as follows. In Section 2, we describe our numerical models of dense star clusters. In Section 3, we discuss the role of the mass segregation and black hole heating. In Section 4, we discuss the role of the primordial binary fraction and pairing on the retention of neutron stars, while, in Section 5, we present the population of compact object binaries containing neutron stars, that we find in our simulations. In Section 6, we summarize our findings and draw our conclusions.

## 2. N-Body Models

We utilize direct N-body evolutionary models of star clusters, computed using the most up-to-date version of Nbody7(Aarseth, 2012). The updates include the treatment of stellar winds, the prescriptions for the natal kicks imparted to remnants at formation and the natal BH spins, the inclusions of relativistic recoil kicks as a result of BH mergers, and the prescriptions for star-star and star-remnant mergers. For details see Banerjee (2020).

The cluster models we consider represent young massive and open clusters that continue to form and dissolve throughout gas-rich galaxies, as in our Galaxy and the Local Group. The star clusters we model have initial sizes of the order of a parsec, consistent with gas-free young clusters in the Milky Way and neighbouring galaxies (Portegies Zwart et al., 2010; Banerjee & Kroupa, 2017)1.

Footnote 1: These clusters are assumed to have survived their assembling and violent-relaxation phases, and have expanded to parsec scale sizes from sub-parsec sizes, as observed in newly-formed, gas-embedded, and partially-embedded clusters and associations (e.g., Banerjee & Kroupa, 2018).

The initial model clusters have Plummer (1911) profiles with masses 1.0\\times 10^{4} M{}_{\\odot}-1.0\\times 10^{5} M{}_{\\odot}, half-mass radii 1.0 pc-3.0 pc, and metallicities 0.0001-0.02. All the computed models are taken to be initially in dynamical equilibrium and unsegregated, subjected to a solar-neighbourhood-like external galactic field (see Table C1 in Banerjee, 2020).

Initial stellar masses are sampled from a Kroupa (2001) initial mass function, in the range 0.08 M{}_{\\odot}-150 M{}_{\\odot}. The overall primordial binary fraction in our models is set to 0.0, 0.05, 0.10. The initial binary fraction of the OB-type stars, of ZAMS mass m_{\\rm ZAMS}\\geq m_{\\rm crit}=16 M{}_{\\odot}, which is taken separately, is \\sim 100 % (Banerjee, 2018), to be consistent with the observed high binary fraction among the OB-type stars in young clusters and associations (see, e.g., Sana & Evans, 2011; Moe & Di Stefano, 2017). In two of the models, we vary the critical mass and fix it to m_{\\rm crit}=5 M{}_{\\odot}(see Banerjee, 2020). For m_{\\rm ZAMS}\\geq m_{\\rm crit}, we pair an OB-star only with another OB-star, as it is typically observed, and the pairing among the lower mass stars is obtained separately. The m_{\\rm ZAMS}\\geq m_{\\rm crit} binaries are taken to initially follow the orbital-period distribution of Sana & Evans (2011) and a uniform mass-ratio distribution. The orbital periods of the lower mass primordial binaries follow the Duquennoy & Mayor (1991) distribution and their mass-ratio distribution is also taken to be uniform. The initial binary eccentricities are drawn from a thermal distribution (Spitzer, 1987) for the binaries with components m_{\\rm ZAMS}<m_{\\rm crit} and from the Sana & Evans (2011) distribution for the m_{\\rm ZAMS}\\geq m_{\\rm crit} binaries.

For what concerns stellar evolution and the formation of compact objects, we consider both pair instability and pulsation pair instability supernovae for BH formation in our simulations (Belczynski et al., 2016). Moreover, we include models where BHs and NSs are born as a result of rapid and delayed supernova (SN; Fryer et al., 2012). In our simulations, the maximum possible NS mass is about 2.5 M{}_{\\odot}. The amount and fraction of the supernova material fallback are provided by the chosen remnant-mass scheme. The remnant natal kick is taken to be Maxwellian with mean velocity \\sigma\\sim 265 km s{}^{-1}, based on observed kick distribution of Galactic NSs (Hobbs et al., 2005). However, NSs that are born in the electron-capture supernova (ECS) are assumed to have a natal kick of the order of \\sim 5 km s{}^{-1}(Podsiadlowski et al., 2004). BH natal kicks are assigned either assuming momentum conservation (Fryer & Kalogera, 2001) or collapse asymmetry (Burrows & Hayes, 1996; Fryer, 2004). For full details see Banerjee et al. (2020) and Banerjee (2020).

All models are evolved until 11 Gyr, unless the cluster is dissolved earlier2.

Footnote 2: The present model clusters initiate and remain over most of their evolution well tidally under-filled, so that they have long dissolution times, >11 Gyr for most of them (Banerjee, 2017, see). Therefore, they continue to exhibit significant dynamical activities for long evolutionary times which is why most models are run up to 11 Gyr (Banerjee, 2020).

## 3. Neutron Star Segregation and Black Hole Heating

NSs form from the collapse of massive stars within the first \\sim 100 Myr of the cluster lifetime. In low-metallicity environments, both the lower and the upper ZAMS (zero-age main sequence) limits of NS formation are smaller than those at high metallicities, thus leading to NS formation over longer evolutionary times. The NSs that form as a result of a core-collapse SN leave the host cluster shortly after their birth, since the cluster escape speed is typically much lower than the natal kick velocity they receive. Only in nuclear star clusters, where the escape velocity is of the same order as \\sigma, a non-negligible fraction of core-collapse NSs could remain bound (see e.g.,Banerjee 2017, 2018a,b). The bulk of the NS population that remains bound to the host star cluster is mainly produced via ECS, since they receive a small kick at birth.

Although the retained NSs are, on average, heavier and remain more concentrated than regular stars, their mass segregation is also in part quenched due to the BH-burning process (Fragione et al., 2018b; Kremer et al., 2020a). Initially, when the BH-engine is active, strong dynamical encounters among the BHs act as a energy source for the rest of the cluster. Moreover, when a large BH population remains in a cluster, the cluster exhibits a large observed core radius (Kremer et al., 2018). For lower metallicities, the BHs could become more massive and numerous, resulting in higher energy deposition in the rest of the cluster members. As the BH population erodes and the engine is weakened, the cluster core radius shrinks and the NSs start efficiently segregate towards the densest regions of the cluster. If the BHs are almost fully depleted, the host cluster attain a structure that would observationally be identified as core-collapsed, with the NSs occupying the central regions (Kremer et al., 2018; Ye et al., 2020).

Figure 1 illustrates the evolution of the half-mass radius distance from the center of the host cluster as a function of time for luminous stars (top panel), NSs (central panel), and BHs (bottom panel), for the cluster models presented in Banerjee (2020). The spikes in the BH distributions come from slow escaping BHs due to evaporation or slow ejections as a consequence of dynamical interactions with other BHs. The spikes in the NS panels are from fluctuations due to the small number of remaining objects close to the cluster's dissolution in a few of the models, typically, among the lowest metallicity and least dense models. We show both cluster models where momentum-conserving natal kicks (left panel) and collapse-asymmetry-driven natal kicks (right panel) are assumed. We find that there is no a significant difference between these two cases, that is the adopted scheme for natal kicks do not affect the dynamical behavior of the NS and BH population.

Roughly independently from the cluster model, Figure 1 shows that the distribution of the NS population typically overlaps with the stellar population. However, NSs do not overlap significantly with the BH population in the innermost cluster regions, which are the most favorable place to form NS-NS and BH-NS binaries owing to their high densities. As discussed first in Fragione et al. (2018), the NS population always approximately maps the stellar population, in the sense that the ratio of the average NS distance from the cluster centre and the average stellar distance from the cluster centre decreases from nearly unity to \\sim 0.6-0.8 in a few Gyrs in all the clusters, almost independently of the initial metallicity, binary fraction, and cluster mass. However, the initial cluster metallicity can play a role (shown in color-code in Figure 1). BHs in metal-rich clusters are of lower mass and do not inject as much energy into the BH-burning process as more massive BHs in metal-poor clusters (Kremer et al., 2020). As a consequence, NSs can segregate more efficiently into the dense cluster core, resulting in a smaller NS half-mass radius and a larger spatial overlap with the BH population.

## 4. Primordial Binary Fraction and Retention of Neutron Stars

In our models, the initial binary fraction of the OB-type stars, of ZAMS mass m_{\\rm ZAMS}\\geq m_{\\rm crit}, is assumed to be \\sim 100\\,\\%(Banerjee, 2018), in order to be consistent with the observed high binary fraction among the massive stars in young clusters and associations (see, e.g., Sana & Evans, 2011; Moe & Di Stefano, 2017). As described in Section 2, most of the our models have m_{\\rm crit}=16\\,\\rm M_{\\odot}. However, the primordial binary fraction and distribution could potentially shape the NS retention and mass spectrum at birth. To preliminarily investigate this, we additionally run cluster models with three different values of the critical mass, m_{\\rm crit}=5\\,\\rm M_{\\odot}, 10\\,\\rm M_{\\odot}, 16\\,\\rm M_{\\odot}, above which the primordial binary fraction is assumed to be \\sim 100\\,\\%. The initial cluster mass, half-mass radius, and metallicity are fixed to M_{\\rm cl}=1.0\\times 10^{5}\\,\\rm M_{\\odot}, r_{\\rm h}=1.5\\,\\rm pc, and Z=0.001, respectively.

In the top panel of Figure 2, we show the mass distribution of NSs retained in the cluster at 120 Myr 4, for different choices of the critical mass. For m_{\\rm crit}=10\\,\\rm M_{\\odot} and 16\\,\\rm M_{\\odot}, the majority of the NS-progenitor stars are single (for the chosen Z=0.001). Therefore, all the core-collapse NSs would typically receive natal kicks high enough (from a Maxwellian distribution with dispersion of \\sim 265\\,\\rm km\\,s^{-1}) to escape the host cluster. Only ECS-NSs receive natal kicks sufficiently low (from a Maxwellian distribution with a dispersion of \\sim 5\\,\\rm km\\,s^{-1}) to be retained. As a result, the NSs retained in the cluster at birth will mostly be of \\sim 1.26\\,\\rm M_{\\odot}(Podsiadlowski et al., 2004), the mass assumed for ECS-NSs in these models, explaining the roughly delta-function mass distribution of NSs with these values of m_{\\rm crit} in Figure 2.

Footnote 4: By this time, NS formation is complete, while dynamics has not significantly shaped NS evolution.

If the critical mass is fixed to m_{\\rm crit}=5\\,\\rm M_{\\odot}, then, initially, all NS progenitors would have a close binary companion with mass \\geq 5.0\\,\\rm M_{\\odot}, i.e. most of them will have an NS-progenitor companion (given the assumed standard stellar initial-mass function). Hence, many of the progenitors of ECS-NSs (that are formed latest from a relatively narrow range of progenitor masses) would escape the cluster when the binary they belong disrupts due to the SN mass loss of the companion. In this case, the components of the binary would be released with speeds of the order of the binary's orbital speed (Kalogera, 1996), which is much larger than cluster escape speed. Whenever the binary is not disrupted, they can also escape along

